cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use rocq-deduxit in your work, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: rocq-deduxit
abstract: >-
  The Rocq library of deduxit, a collection of logic suites, against
  which the certificates deduxit writes are checked. For ordinary logic:
  the calculi of intuitionistic and minimal logic as inductives, their
  Kripke models and a checker of finite countermodels that proves a
  sequent underivable; and first-order intuitionistic, minimal and
  classical logic, with LJ's and LM's soundness for Kripke models with
  growing domains, LK's for finite structures, and checkers of both
  kinds of countermodel. For linear logic: the one-sided sequent
  calculus with Mix, weakening and cut as parameters, the two-sided
  calculus of intuitionistic linear logic and its affine variant, and
  their first-order versions, as inductives; deduxit's proof terms as
  datatypes, their checkers as functions and the theorems that a term a
  checker accepts proves its sequent; the invariants every derivation
  keeps, which give certificates that a sequent is not derivable; and
  the reduction of Mix to ?(⊥⊗⊥). For modal logic: the labelled sequent
  calculus of the systems from K to S5, its soundness for Kripke
  models, and checkers of labelled proofs and of finite countermodels.
  Its definitions,
  proofs, tests and documentation were written with substantial AI
  assistance, by Anthropic's Claude models in Claude Code, in steps the
  author planned, directed and accepted, and the author is responsible
  for them. Rocq's kernel checks every proof, and no theorem rests on an
  axiom: each one's assumptions are closed under the global context.
